---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Which hosts run signed exact-ref models in Traverse?"
  description="Discover → execute → trace on one shared runtime.wasm. v0.15.0 exact-ref: native, web, Swift swift-host-v0.15.0, Kotlin and .NET 0.15.0. model_usage is required. No host is Certified."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/which-hosts-run-signed-exact-ref-models.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> discover → execute → trace, on one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>. As of <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.15.0">v0.15.0</a>, published exact-ref execute is native Rust, the web embedder, Swift, Kotlin, and .NET. Pin by digest and signature. A naked URL is not identity. No host is Certified under Spec 529.</p>

  <h2>Honest matrix (checked 14 October 2026)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Native Rust</strong> — <code>ExactModelHostConnector</code> in the v0.15.0 crates. <code>model_usage</code> is required.</li>
    <li><strong>Web</strong> — published <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.15.0</code> executes exact-ref (<code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code>). Pass <code>modelUsage</code> whenever pins exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Swift</strong> — <code>ExactModelHost</code> is in the published xcframework <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/swift-host-v0.15.0"><code>swift-host-v0.15.0</code></a>. <code>Package.swift</code> on main pins its rebuild <code>swift-host-v0.15.0-1</code>, which adds the Spec 138 guest reject codes (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1695">#1695</a>). The v0.15.0 tag's <code>Package.swift</code> still pointed at <code>swift-host-v0.14.0-4</code>. Distribution is GitHub Release + SPM binary target.</li>
    <li><strong>Kotlin</strong> — Maven Central <code>com.traverse-framework:traverse-embedder</code> 0.15.0 includes <code>ExactModelHost</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1580">#1580</a>, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1651">#1651</a> closed). The 0.14.0 AAR did not.</li>
    <li><strong>.NET</strong> — nuget.org <code>TraverseEmbedder</code> 0.15.0 includes <code>ExactModelHost</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1602">#1602</a>, #1651 closed). The 0.14.0 package did not. Native engine RIDs in the release: <code>win-x64</code>, <code>win-arm64</code>, <code>linux-x64</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rights, ONNX, and Core ML</h2>
  <p>Every host enforces model rights. Declaring <code>model_usage</code> is breaking: omit it and the call fails closed with <code>usage_undeclared</code>. Third-party ONNX models can be packaged, signed, and verified with <code>traverse-cli model</code>. Apple hosts can opt in to a signed Core ML variant; the default placement stays <code>wasm-cpu</code>. Evidence so far is macOS <code>cargo test</code> plus the Decision 113 iPhone spike. Governed-path iPhone evidence is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1630">traverse#1630</a>, still open.</p>

  <h2>Signing honesty</h2>
  <p>Packages use schema <code>2.0.0</code> and later, plus detached Ed25519 <code>model.sig.json</code>. Host-owned trust roots gate <code>register_package</code> / <code>registerPackage</code>. The first trained package <code>digits-mlp-1.0.0</code> is signed with a <strong>test-only</strong> key. Production custody, rotation, and revocation are still <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1567">#1567</a> — see <a href="/questions/is-production-model-signing-ready.html">Is production model signing ready?</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>Not a claim that any host is Certified. Not a web, Android, or desktop GPU adapter. Not Spec 026 registry-capability revocation, which is still <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1598">traverse#1598</a>. Deeper loop: <a href="/questions/what-is-exact-ref-model-execution.html">What is exact-ref model execution?</a>.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
